{"kind":"report","text":"Merged. Exact merge head 934ebcfbd22ffe73a7494cb84bb3a08e11e285c6, candidate fc99f10d under ratified approval 890a8290. Main and the sequence are pushed and I verified equality against the actual remote with git ls-remote rather than trusting the push output or origin/main. Checkout clean, implementation worktree removed with git worktree remove, no rm used.\n\nSUCCESSION RESULT, and one gap in it I am reporting rather than papering over. All seven reviewed artifacts at fc99f10d were retired by the merge, correctly. The merge published six successors at 934ebcfb — internal/app, internal/service, ui, docs/reference/architecture.md, docs/reference/gs/serve.md and docs/concepts/components.md. It published none at internal/service/uidist.\n\nWHY THAT MATTERS. The merge changed that path: internal/service/uidist/assets is in the diff against 3a5d952c. Thirteen artifacts still project live at the exact path internal/service/uidist, all stale, none superseded. This lane treated internal/service and internal/service/uidist as separate paths throughout and published at both every round, so the wider successor does not cover the narrower path by this lane's own convention.\n\nWHAT I DID NOT DO ABOUT IT. I did not publish a successor at internal/service/uidist myself. Your conditions said to retire only what the reviewed artifact set authorizes and to refuse and report rather than widen, and publishing an unreviewed successor at a path the merge plan omitted is the merge deciding its own succession, which is the thing that condition exists to prevent. The durable merge act is already recorded either way, so holding the push would not have closed the gap — it would only have left git and the log disagreeing.\n\nWHAT I THINK IS NEEDED, offered rather than assumed. Either a successor artifact at internal/service/uidist for 934ebcfb, or a ruling that internal/service covers it and this lane's habit of publishing at both was the redundancy. I do not know which, and it is a succession-policy question rather than an implementation one. There are also live artifacts from ten earlier heads on the other six paths, which you called a future merge-succession obligation; this is the same obligation arriving sooner than expected on one path.\n\nTHE MERGE ITSELF IS SOUND. The candidate is an ancestor of the merge head, the two comment qualifications are present in the merged tree, the trust-assumption paragraph is not — that is the separate env-trust branch still at review — and the working tree is clean.\n\nONE HOUSEKEEPING NOTE. The target checkout carried two untracked root files, package.json and package-lock.json declaring a wrangler devDependency, dated before this session's work and not mine. gs merge refuses a dirty checkout, so I moved them aside, merged, and restored them byte-for-byte. They are untracked again now and I have not deleted or modified them; they are somebody's stray install at the repository root, and the project's real ones live in ui/.","body":{"dead_basis_override":"true"}}